From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gregory Mitchell <me@gmitch215.xyz>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 20:58:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] wasm: tell the host when a yielding task has a signal waiting

A task in a user-mode loop never enters the kernel: the host runs it
until its fuel runs out and gives the cpu to another task, and on the
way back no return from an interrupt delivers its pending signals. A
thread spinning in for (;;) could not be cancelled asynchronously and
never saw a signal another thread sent it (libc-test pthread_cancel
hung). wasm_user_work_pending reports whether the current task has
signal work, so the host can pass it through the kernel's exit to
user mode at the yield.
---
include/asm-generic/thread_info_tif.h:13:#define _TIF_SIGPENDING		BIT(TIF_SIGPENDING)
include/asm-generic/thread_info_tif.h:16:#define _TIF_NOTIFY_SIGNAL	BIT(TIF_NOTIFY_SIGNAL)
157:#define read_thread_flags() \
diff --git a/arch/wasm/kernel/signal.c b/arch/wasm/kernel/signal.c
index 85c3d2917..dc928068f 100644
--- a/arch/wasm/kernel/signal.c
+++ b/arch/wasm/kernel/signal.c
@@ -166,6 +166,15 @@ static void handle_signal(struct ksignal *ksig, struct pt_regs *regs)
 	signal_setup_done(ret, ksig, 0);
 }
 
+/*
+ * For the host when a task yields in user mode: whether passing through the kernel would deliver
+ * a signal, as the return from a timer interrupt would on another architecture.
+ */
+__visible unsigned long wasm_user_work_pending(void)
+{
+	return read_thread_flags() & (_TIF_SIGPENDING | _TIF_NOTIFY_SIGNAL);
+}
+
 void arch_do_signal_or_restart(struct pt_regs *regs)
 {
 	struct ksignal ksig;
